{% set local_problem %}
<p>Many Huntsville homes have a downspout problem they don't even know about. Builder-grade downspouts installed during Huntsville's rapid growth periods (especially the 2000s and 2010s construction booms in Hampton Cove, Jones Valley, and South Huntsville) were often the minimum size code allows. They're fine for light rain, but completely overwhelmed during North Alabama's intense spring storms.</p>

<p>When downspouts can't keep up with the volume of water your roof collects, gutters back up and overflow. Even worse, many downspouts simply dump water right next to the foundation — a few inches from your home at best. On Huntsville's infamous red clay soil, that water doesn't absorb. It pools, finds the path of least resistance, and eventually makes its way into your basement or crawlspace.</p>

<p>Historic homes in Twickenham and Five Points face different challenges: century-old drainage systems designed for a different era, downspouts that have been patched and modified over decades, and complex rooflines that require careful water management.</p>

<p>Whether you're in a new subdivision or a historic neighborhood, the solution is the same: properly sized downspouts, correctly positioned, with extensions that move water well away from your foundation.</p>
{% endset %}

{% set service_explanation %}
<p>A good downspout system has three essential components: adequate capacity, proper positioning, and effective water dispersal.</p>

<p>Capacity means having enough downspouts, sized appropriately for your roof area and Huntsville's rainfall intensity. We often recommend 3x4 inch downspouts instead of standard 2x3 inch — they handle the volume during heavy storms without backing up or causing gutter overflow.</p>

<p>Positioning means placing downspouts where water naturally flows, typically at corners and low points in your gutter system. Poorly placed downspouts create dead zones where water pools instead of draining.</p>

<p>Water dispersal means getting that water far from your foundation. Basic splash blocks move water a few inches. Extensions move it a few feet. For Huntsville's clay soil, underground drains that route water 10+ feet away provide the best protection — and they're invisible once installed.</p>
{% endset %}

